<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The frozen table</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The idea is simple enough to state in one paragraph. Take an existing embedding matrix E (we use Gemma 3 270M’s own, 262,144 by 640). Centre it, take the top 512 principal directions W, and keep only the sign of each projection:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">code(t) = sign( W^T (E_t - mu) ) in {-1, +1}^512</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">That is 512 bits per token, 16.8 MB for the whole vocabulary, and it never changes again. Going in, a token’s code passes through a small trained linear map (512 to 640) into the body. Coming out, the body’s final state passes through another small map (640 to 512) and is scored against every code by a dot product, divided by sqrt(512). The two maps are 0.65M parameters. A fixed per-token prior, the log unigram frequency of each token in the training data, is added to the scores; it costs 1 MB and has no trainable parameters. We call it the seal. Arm C lets this prior be trained from that starting point, which adds 262,144 trainable numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The table keeps most of what the pretrained embedding knew. The 512 directions carry 92.3% of E’s variance. On WordSim-353, the codes’ agreement correlates with human similarity ratings at 0.617, against 0.614 for the float embedding itself. Of each token’s 10 nearest float neighbours, 67% are among its 10 nearest code neighbours.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Because the table is a function of E, any vector in E’s space can become a token after training: a new word gets code(mean of its pieces’ rows), or code of any vector you choose. A learned table has no such rule. A new row in it is random until it is trained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiment</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Body.</span> Gemma 3 270M’s architecture exactly, built from the public configuration with random initialisation: 18 layers, width 640, gated GELU FFN of 2,048, 4 query and 1 key-value head of size 256 with QK-norm, five local (512-token sliding window) layers per global layer, RoPE base 10k local and 1M global, RMSNorm, no biases, no dropout. We trained from scratch in every arm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Arms.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Arm A.</span> The learned tied table, 262,144 by 640, with the body in bfloat16 autocast. This is the model as Google would train it, at our data scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Arm B.</span> The frozen table and the fixed seal, same float body.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Arm C.</span> The frozen table, every linear layer in the body replaced by BitNet b1.58’s BitLinear (RMSNorm on the input, int8 absmax activations per token, absmean ternary weights, straight-through estimator) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, attention arithmetic and KV cache in 16-bit as in BitNet, and the seal trained from its unigram start. A has 268.1M trainable parameters; B 101.0M; C 101.2M.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data and schedule.</span> 2.05B tokens of FineWeb-Edu (sample-10BT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> tokenised with Gemma’s tokenizer, documents separated by the end-of-sequence token, 10M held-out validation tokens. Sequence length 1,024; 262,144 tokens per optimiser step; 7,820 steps. Every arm draws exactly the same batches in the same order from the same seed. A and B use AdamW with peak learning rate 6e-4, betas 0.9 and 0.95, weight decay 0.1, 1% warm-up and cosine decay to 10%. C uses the recipe BitNet’s authors published for their smallest model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>: peak 1.5e-3 decaying linearly, dropping to a 1e-3 peak at the midpoint, weight decay 0.1 in the first half and 0 in the second, 375 warm-up steps. We did not tune anything for C; we took their numbers. This means C differs from B in three ways (body, schedule, trained prior), which we return to in Section 6.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost.</span> Each arm trained on one RTX 5090 in 14 to 16 hours, about 18 US dollars per arm at the rental price we paid. The whole study, including evaluation, cost under 75 dollars.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span> Validation loss on 512 held-out sequences of 1,024 tokens. LAMBADA, HellaSwag, ARC-Easy and PIQA with the lm-evaluation-harness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, zero-shot, full test sets. Every per-question result is saved, and every error bar in this paper is a 95% bootstrap range over those questions (10,000 re-draws). Differences between arms are paired on the same questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
