<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What the learned table buys.</span> In loss, 0.20 nats, which is about 23% lower perplexity. On the benchmarks, much less. B is better than A on LAMBADA by 1.6 points, tied on HellaSwag and PIQA, and worse on ARC-Easy by 3.4 points. That is the whole return on 168M trained parameters: one benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How the gap develops.</span> Figure 1 shows validation loss over training, checked every 200 steps. B starts ahead: at step 200 it is 0.23 nats below A, because its codes already carry Gemma’s meanings and its prior already knows word frequencies. A overtakes at step 600, pulls ahead until about step 3,000, and from there the gap stays at 0.20 to the end. B’s loss falls at every check. The learned table’s advantage is not a head start that fades; it is a capacity that A grows into once it has seen enough data to train the table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Where the gap is.</span> Table 4 splits the validation loss by how often the target token appeared in training. The learned table wins in the middle: tokens seen between 100 and 100,000 times, where there are enough examples to train a row and the row then beats a fixed code. On the 161 most common tokens, which are 52% of all text, the gap is 0.035 nats. On tokens seen 10 to 99 times, the frozen table wins by 2.3 nats (B) and 1.7 nats (C): the learned rows never got enough examples, and the codes arrived knowing the word.</p>
</div>
