<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Of Gemma’s 262,144 tokens, 52,782 never appear in our 2.05B training tokens and only 147,187 appear ten or more times. In A, the rows of the rest are random or barely moved from random. In B and C they still carry Gemma’s codes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The ternary model.</span> C is about 40 MB at 1.58 bits per weight: 16.8 MB of table, about 20 MB of ternary body, 1.3 MB of 16-bit interface maps and 0.5 MB of prior. The file we actually run (next paragraph) packs the body at 2 bits per weight and keeps the maps, norms and scales in 32-bit, and is 46.2 MB. A in 16-bit is 543 MB as a GGUF file; B would be about 220 MB. C ties A on LAMBADA and PIQA, is 0.9 points behind on HellaSwag and 4.9 behind on ARC-Easy. Against B, which has the same table and a float body, C is within 0.8 to 2.0 points everywhere and 0.08 nats in loss. BitNet’s two-stage schedule behaved as its authors describe: C’s loss curve is S-shaped, and at the learning-rate drop at step 3,911 it fell 0.036 nats in 200 steps, 3.6 times B’s fall over the same steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On a laptop CPU.</span> We wrote a CPU kernel for C (released with the paper: one C file, AVX2, ternary matrix-vector products with int8 activations ported from our SHADOW-50M kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and a readout that scores all 262,144 codes straight off the 1-bit table) and ran A through llama.cpp from its GGUF files, on the same machine, the same 8 threads, both with a 16-bit KV cache. Table 5 gives the result. The kernel reproduces the PyTorch model: on 64 validation tokens its argmax agrees at every position and its next-token loss is 4.495 against 4.483. There is no kernel for B, whose float body is the same as A’s; it would sit between the two.</p>
</div>
