<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The 8-bit GGUF loses nothing (23.864 against 23.857, far inside the error), so the fair opponent for C is A at Q8_0: C generates 5.3 to 6.4 times faster in 7.6 times less memory. A cannot get below 446 MB at any quantisation because llama.cpp, like every other quantiser, keeps the 262,144 by 640 table in 16-bit: 250 MB of the 4-bit file is the vocabulary. C’s speed is set by memory traffic, not arithmetic: each generated token streams the 25 MB body and the 17 MB table through the processor, and this laptop’s memory moves 38 GB/s, so its ceiling is about 860 tokens per second; a processor whose last-level cache holds the 46 MB would go faster. C’s quality is lower than A’s by the 0.28 nats of Table 2; that is the trade the table makes, and the benchmarks in Table 3 say how much of it is visible.</p>
</div>
