<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Adding to the vocabulary after training</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A learned table cannot take a new token without training: the new row is random. A frozen table can, because its codes come from a rule. We tested what that is worth, and we report the negatives with the positives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A domain vocabulary, no training.</span> We took PubMed articles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, found the 10,000 most frequent biomedical words that Gemma’s tokenizer splits into two or more pieces (2.5 pieces on average), and added each as a single new token: for B and C, code(mean of its pieces’ Gemma rows) with a prior set to the word’s estimated training frequency, chosen on a validation split; for A, a new row at the mean norm of its learned rows, the fair untrained equivalent. On 300 held-out articles the new tokens replace 3.2% of all tokens. Table 6 shows the cost.</p>
</div>
