<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The learned table collapses. The frozen table takes 10,000 new words at a 3% cost and reads the text with 3% fewer tokens. We tried several ways of setting the new tokens’ priors and several ways of building their codes (mean of pieces, first piece, a code read off the model’s own output before the word); none made the cost zero. The model’s trained reading of the pieces is slightly better than any code we could construct for the whole word. So the honest claim is about possibility, not improvement: a frozen table accepts new rows and a learned table does not. The improvement case is the one our SHADOW-50M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> met in practice, a table that could not spell 8,600 English pieces at all; Gemma’s vocabulary leaves almost nothing of that kind to add (0.001% of held-out tokens fall back to bytes).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tokens the model never trained on.</span> On text in 20 languages, B is better than A overall in Arabic, Bulgarian, Spanish, Russian and Vietnamese and worse in the other fifteen. Tokens that never occurred in training are penalised in B by the seal, which gives a count of zero the prior log(1/N). Setting those tokens’ prior to the median of the seen tokens, with no training, makes B better than A on never-seen tokens in Thai, Arabic, Russian, French and Greek; A stays ahead in Hindi, Japanese and Chinese. The codes carry meaning for tokens the model never saw; the prior has to be told not to forbid them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What did not work.</span> Two tests we expected to favour the frozen table did not. Brand-new control tokens (<span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;tool_call&gt;</span>, <span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span>, <span id="S5.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;sql&gt;</span>, citation and field markers, five families, 24 prompt rotations each) are placed correctly from in-context examples by every arm: at five shots, B 88%, C 75%, A 73%; at one shot A is highest, from rows that happened to score high with no examples. That test measures in-context learning, which every arm has, and the luck of the random row. Emoji prediction for tweets (SemEval-2018 task 2, 20 classes) is at chance for all three models, 2.6 to 5.7% against 5%; a model trained on 2B English tokens has no tweet-to-emoji knowledge for a code to unlock. Both results are released with the rest.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One seed per arm. The error bars are over test questions, not over training runs; a second seed could move the loss gap by a few hundredths. Two billion tokens is Chinchilla-optimal for the 101M-parameter arms and under-trains A’s table; it is also the budget we had. The learned table’s advantage grows with data per token, so the loss gap at 20B tokens is likely larger than 0.20, and the benchmark picture may change with it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">C differs from B in three things, not one: the ternary body, BitNet’s schedule, and the trained prior. We measured the prior’s share in a separate run of B with the trained prior that we stopped at step 1,833 for budget: it was 0.003 to 0.022 nats better than B at every matched check. The schedule’s share we did not isolate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The frozen codes are derived from Gemma 3 270M’s embedding, so they inherit whatever that embedding knows and does not know, and a release of the table carries Google’s Gemma terms. The method needs some pretrained embedding to start from; an alternative is to train a small model with a learned table once and freeze its codes for everything after, which we have not tested here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Adding vocabulary after training costs quality in every setting we tried where the tokenizer already covered the words. The gain is bounded by what the tokenizer is missing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The CPU comparison is one laptop and two different code bases: our kernel for C against llama.cpp for A. Both are memory-bound, so the ratio should hold on other machines while the absolute numbers scale with memory bandwidth, but we measured only the one machine. B has no kernel. Our kernel’s prefill is one token at a time, so we compare generation speed only.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our own SHADOW-250M and SHADOW-50M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> are the origin of the frozen 512-bit table and of the CPU kernels used here; this paper supplies the controlled comparison those releases did not have. Bochkov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> trains transformers from scratch with frozen binary codes derived from token identity on the input and a trained float output head; the codes carry no meaning and the output table stays. Our codes come from a pretrained embedding, are shared by input and output, and leave no vocabulary matrix on either side. Press and Wolf <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> tie input and output embeddings; ALBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> factorises the table into a small learned one and a projection; hash embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> share rows between tokens. All keep the table trainable and float. Meister et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> initialise a per-token output bias to the log unigram distribution and train it, which is what our arm C does and arm B freezes. BitNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> gives us the ternary body and the schedule; the BitNet papers report model memory excluding the embedding. GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> quantise the body after training and leave the embedding in 16-bit. FineWeb-Edu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> is the training data, Gemma 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> the architecture and the source embedding, and the straight-through estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> the training trick for ternary weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>What we release</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Everything the paper used, at the repository above: the training script with every setting; the frozen table, the unigram counts and the seal; the 2.05B training tokens and the validation tokens with checksums; all intermediate and final checkpoints of A, B and C (weights and optimiser state, every 800 steps) and the partial run of B with the trained prior; the per-step training logs; the evaluation scripts and every per-question benchmark result, so any number in this paper can be recomputed, and any other arm can be added to the same tables. Licence: non-commercial research. The table and the checkpoints of B and C contain codes derived from Gemma 3 270M and are released under Google’s Gemma terms as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>What comes next</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A table that is a rule rather than a trained matrix does not have to stop at words. The next paper puts one frozen table under text, images, audio and video at once: every kind of input and output lands in the same 512-bit code space, so one small body reads a picture, hears a sound and writes a sentence with no learned embedding anywhere, and the whole thing still fits on a CPU. The teaser is all we will say here; the measurements come with the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
